==English==
{{was wotd|2011|July|4}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂ewg-}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|auteur||author}}. Popularised by {{w|François Truffaut}} in the 1954 essay “Une certaine tendance du cinéma français” (“A certain tendency in French cinema”) in the influential film journal ''{{w|Cahiers du Cinéma}}'' as the phrase “la politique des Auteurs”. {{doublet|en|author}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GenAm}} {{IPA|en|/oʊˈtɝ/}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ɔːˈtɜː/|/əʊˈtɜː/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-auteur.wav|Audio (UK)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɜː(ɹ)|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|au|teur}}
* {{homophones|en|hauteur}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A creative [[artist]], especially a [[film director]], seen as having a specific, recognisable artistic [[vision]], and who is seen as the single or [[preeminent]] ‘[[author]]’ of his works.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=11 Feb 1974||author=William Bender|title=Call to vespers|work=Time|passage=The libretto was a piece of hack work from a Parisian scenario factory run by an enterprising '''auteur''' of sorts named Eugene Scribe.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=24 Apr 2003|work=The Economist|title=Broadway is bigger than ever|url=https://www.economist.com/books-and-arts/2003/04/24/broadway-is-bigger-than-ever|passage=Since Mr Luhrmann first tackled the opera, he has entered the select circle of celebrity directors on the basis of only three films, including “Moulin Rouge”. And his “La Bohème”—designed by Mrs Luhrmann, Catherine Martin, a double Oscar-winner for “Moulin Rouge”—is avowedly the work of an '''''auteur'''''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Rosanna Maule|title=Beyond Auteurism: New Directions in Authorial Film Practices in France, Italy and Spain Since the 1980s|year=2008|publisher=Intellect Books|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=VbC8vKJgbRYC&pg=PA90&dq=%22cin%C3%A9ma+du+look%22&hl=&cd=6&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22cin%C3%A9ma%20du%20look%22&f=false|isbn=978-1-84150-204-5|page=90|passage=Ginette Vincendeau describes the ''cinéma du look'' as a typical example of ‘mainstream co-optation’ of ‘avant-garde, artisanal, or '''auteur'''’ cinema (1996: 14).}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=23 Jun 2011|title=Terrence Malick to Woody Allen – the directors actors will kill to work for|url=https://www.theguardian.com/film/2011/jun/23/actor-magnets-terrence-malick-brad-pitt|author=Jane Graham|work=The Guardian|passage=If a widely respected '''auteur''' such as Martin Scorsese, Allen or Malick has given you the stamp of approval, you might not live fast or die young, but you'll leave a good-looking legacy.}}

